1
00:00:01,070 --> 00:00:01,710
Hi everyone.

2
00:00:02,490 --> 00:00:09,130
In this video our talk will be about a homemade machine for winding multi-turn coils of the Tesla transformer type.

3
00:00:10,350 --> 00:00:15,150
All who have ever wound them know what a laborious process this is and how much time it takes.

4
00:00:16,410 --> 00:00:24,150
For example, to wind such a Tesla on a 50mm frame in terms of time, the work takes about an hour of intensive labor.

5
00:00:25,850 --> 00:00:31,530
Making it in manual mode is quite simple because such a diameter easily fits in the hand.

6
00:00:32,610 --> 00:00:43,150
But when it comes to winding such coils, here the frame diameter is 153mm and besides if it's varnish coated, doing this manually is already very problematic.

7
00:00:44,950 --> 00:00:48,550
I made this build in a couple of evenings, counting the time the glue dried.

8
00:00:50,670 --> 00:01:00,250
The machine itself is made from solid pine, with 20cm and the length from the front rack of the machine to the end of the lower guide is 80cm.

9
00:01:00,750 --> 00:01:06,350
The front rack of the machine is fastened rigidly, and the rear rack can move along the lower guide.

10
00:01:06,810 --> 00:01:09,390
This way we can install frames of different lengths here.

11
00:01:11,390 --> 00:01:20,470
Here from the central hole to the base of the lower guide is 16cm, which can let us install a coil about 30cm in diameter for winding wire.

12
00:01:26,840 --> 00:01:30,640
The work on this build should start with being able to find some motor with a gearbox.

13
00:01:32,760 --> 00:01:37,920
In this case in my stocks I found this, can't say from what, sometime I bought it incidentally.

14
00:01:39,300 --> 00:01:48,300
The main moment here is the gear ratio, here it's 100 to 1, that is 100 motor revolutions correspond to 1 revolution of the central shaft.

15
00:01:48,760 --> 00:01:54,340
The motor here is DC and this is very good, since further you can do rotation regulation here.

16
00:01:56,400 --> 00:01:59,380
The minimum tool set for this build is a jigsaw.

17
00:02:01,440 --> 00:02:09,620
I advise applying small tooth blades on the jigsaw for cutting, they suit very well for cutting plywood, but also the end cut comes out quite quality.

18
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:15,680
Further this is a screwdriver, a set of bits and some set of drills.

19
00:02:17,840 --> 00:02:19,840
Carpenters glue and a bit of patience.

20
00:02:22,300 --> 00:02:25,740
The front rack is glued at a 90 degree angle for us.

21
00:02:26,780 --> 00:02:33,400
It's desirable to keep to this angle, so we wouldn't further have wobbles and curvatures of the central guide rotation axis.

22
00:02:34,700 --> 00:02:40,120
The rear rack is installed on the lower guide also at a 90 degree angle to its movement.

23
00:02:41,100 --> 00:02:46,280
Well, and here you can see I traced lines, that we also have a 90 degree angle here.

24
00:02:50,380 --> 00:02:53,920
The rear rack is also glued, but glued at this point.

25
00:02:57,150 --> 00:02:58,480
Here we have adjustment screws.

26
00:02:59,860 --> 00:03:03,280
The width of the rear rack of the machine, I planed it slightly.

27
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:06,700
That's why it's 3 millimeters narrower than the main guide.

28
00:03:06,700 --> 00:03:11,940
And here certain gaps are visible from one side and the other.

29
00:03:13,200 --> 00:03:21,940
These gaps let me, by tightening these screws, set the support force on the lower guide so this rear rack could move with effort and wouldn't pop out.

30
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:27,680
I fastened it to the lower guide at these holes with screws.

31
00:03:32,120 --> 00:03:35,340
This part seats on carpenters glue, like these guide corners.

32
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:42,400
The central rotation axis of the coil is a 6 millimeters stud with cut thread.

33
00:03:43,040 --> 00:03:48,360
The cut thread along its whole length lets you easily pick the needed distance for installing guide discs.

34
00:03:49,120 --> 00:03:53,620
The guide discs are also cut from solid plywood 9 millimeters thick.

35
00:03:54,600 --> 00:03:57,480
After the disc was cut, I drew it with compass help.

36
00:03:58,700 --> 00:04:03,120
All put on an axis, the axis clamped in a screwdriver and rotates.

37
00:04:03,120 --> 00:04:07,900
The edges passed with sandpaper, that way getting a clear geometric circle.

38
00:04:09,900 --> 00:04:16,880
And with sandpaper we fit these discs under the moment they enter the tube of the future Tesla coil frame with effort.

39
00:04:20,250 --> 00:04:22,170
This is how the build works assembled.

40
00:04:23,630 --> 00:04:30,330
Now I do the revolution number regulation with the help of the lab power supply by changing the supply DC potential.

41
00:04:48,310 --> 00:04:50,170
Now let's move on to some small tricks.

42
00:04:51,230 --> 00:04:55,130
At the end I'll show how a Tesla transformer is wound on this machine.

43
00:04:55,670 --> 00:05:02,610
Before starting to wind wire, cut the frame of necessary length, according to the calculation of winding length on this frame.

44
00:05:03,450 --> 00:05:06,010
And make a hole through which the wire will pass.

45
00:05:06,790 --> 00:05:08,270
This will be the start of the winding.

46
00:05:08,930 --> 00:05:10,630
I made it at this point with all help.

47
00:05:11,910 --> 00:05:16,150
After the hole is made, clean its top with a knife, so there's an even surface.

48
00:05:19,750 --> 00:05:27,450
I recommend making the start hole a couple cm from the edge of the frame, so there's a distance for which it'd be convenient to fix the coil on the board.

49
00:05:30,490 --> 00:05:34,730
And now when the hole is made, we can move on to installing the guide disc.

50
00:05:37,690 --> 00:05:43,210
The front guide disc I've already fastened, and the rear guide disc is in a movable position.

51
00:05:44,390 --> 00:05:48,690
The rear guide disc will install so that this hole that was made is at the front.

52
00:05:50,650 --> 00:05:54,450
After the guide discs are tightened, the coil frame is put in its place.

53
00:05:55,830 --> 00:06:03,770
The front guide disc is put practically at the edge of the coil frame, and the rear is sunk to a depth so the wire hole in the frame was behind it.

54
00:06:08,510 --> 00:06:09,690
I can demonstrate.

55
00:06:10,550 --> 00:06:12,650
The awl enters, and here is the tip.

56
00:06:13,430 --> 00:06:14,710
We'll pull the wire through here.

57
00:06:16,210 --> 00:06:17,870
Two unfastened nuts remain.

58
00:06:18,490 --> 00:06:21,030
This will support in the rear part of the machine rack.

59
00:06:23,190 --> 00:06:27,610
Now here is the moment of the stud that's the rotation axis, and has a through hole.

60
00:06:28,450 --> 00:06:34,450
The same through hole is present in the transitional shaft made from a copper tube of corresponding diameter.

61
00:06:35,490 --> 00:06:45,030
When putting this shaft on the stud, the hole becomes coaxial, and a cotter pin is lowered into the hole, this way preventing rotation of the coupling on the stud.

62
00:06:47,990 --> 00:06:53,570
The stud is round from this side, and from the opposite end I made a shape under the gearbox shaft.

63
00:06:58,700 --> 00:07:00,840
All made from 9mm plywood.

64
00:07:02,300 --> 00:07:06,300
Here is a glued magnet that serves as the revolution sensor on the machine.

65
00:07:08,820 --> 00:07:10,700
It activates a reed switch.

66
00:07:11,840 --> 00:07:16,540
Now I installed the guide axis between the front support rack of the machine and the rear.

67
00:07:17,860 --> 00:07:26,240
The rear support rack is movable, it can move along the machine guide board, that way we can change the installation point under various frame lengths.

68
00:07:27,740 --> 00:07:32,960
I approximately made for myself a winding machine for coils of 80cm length.

69
00:07:33,760 --> 00:07:35,240
This is quite enough for my needs.

70
00:07:36,300 --> 00:07:40,140
Also on the stud I installed a spacer tube that abuts this point.

71
00:07:44,120 --> 00:07:48,660
So the wood didn't break from the thread, I made a washer, seating it on two screws.

72
00:07:51,360 --> 00:07:56,520
The copper spacer tube abuts exactly into it, and then it abuts these spacer nuts.

73
00:07:59,260 --> 00:08:03,360
It's important to accurately strengthen the whole build so it didn't have play.

74
00:08:04,520 --> 00:08:09,920
Because when you're winding wire, if there's play, this will reflect in additional inconveniences.

75
00:08:11,020 --> 00:08:13,800
And so when pressed, the system works stably.

76
00:08:16,910 --> 00:08:21,430
The rear support build of the machine is fastened to the guide with two screws.

77
00:08:23,710 --> 00:08:31,810
In the front and rear part, and analogously from the opposite side, this way the build acquires rigidity and can be installed in all positions.

78
00:08:34,430 --> 00:08:36,790
Here I remove the play with nut's help.

79
00:08:38,510 --> 00:08:40,490
Now axial shift is absent.

80
00:08:42,170 --> 00:08:44,810
You can pay attention to how the reed switch works.

81
00:08:49,160 --> 00:08:54,580
When the magnet approaches, the reed switch contact attracts, and closes the system.

82
00:08:57,900 --> 00:09:02,280
The output of the reed switch through this adapter will be connected to a calculator.

83
00:09:06,830 --> 00:09:07,990
It'll count revolutions.

84
00:09:09,370 --> 00:09:12,450
I did the connection of the calculator to the reed switch.

85
00:09:13,190 --> 00:09:19,370
These two conductors going into the calculator are now connected on the board parallel to the contacts of the equals key.

86
00:09:20,150 --> 00:09:21,330
How does this work?

87
00:09:22,010 --> 00:09:26,270
Turn on the calculator and dial so it counts the revolution count for us.

88
00:09:27,110 --> 00:09:29,070
1 plus 1 and press equals.

89
00:09:33,910 --> 00:09:36,890
The calculator now switched to grand total mode.

90
00:09:36,890 --> 00:09:43,110
And at each press of the equals key, it'll add the last addend to the previous number.

91
00:09:43,890 --> 00:09:45,090
The last addend was 1.

92
00:09:45,810 --> 00:09:48,710
That way, we now at each press will sum plus 1.

93
00:09:51,070 --> 00:09:53,570
Now I turn on the system, apply power.

94
00:10:02,140 --> 00:10:06,500
And you see that on shaft rotation our calculator counts the revolution count.

95
00:10:06,500 --> 00:10:07,420
If

96
00:10:18,100 --> 00:10:23,820
I increase the supply voltage, the revolutions accelerate and the count goes faster.

97
00:10:28,810 --> 00:10:32,440
There's one more feature of using the calculator.

98
00:10:32,760 --> 00:10:38,320
For example, we're winding and our wire sharply went into overlap and we passed some turn count.

99
00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:40,920
And the system continues to count for us.

100
00:10:42,560 --> 00:10:46,500
Turn off power and remember the number of revolutions that was on the calculator.

101
00:10:49,430 --> 00:10:50,930
Now we have 46.

102
00:10:51,730 --> 00:10:52,910
Zero out the indicator.

103
00:10:54,570 --> 00:10:55,950
Now enter it again.

104
00:10:56,650 --> 00:10:57,370
46.

105
00:10:58,810 --> 00:10:59,530
Minus.

106
00:11:00,950 --> 00:11:02,190
And dial 1.

107
00:11:08,120 --> 00:11:12,540
Now unwind the turn count manually back to the spool that went into overlap.

108
00:11:13,700 --> 00:11:16,500
And the calculator at that subtracts the turn count.

109
00:11:17,420 --> 00:11:19,140
I rotate now in manual mode.

110
00:11:20,040 --> 00:11:20,400
45.

111
00:11:22,140 --> 00:11:22,640
Unwind.

112
00:11:23,960 --> 00:11:24,680
44.

113
00:11:28,620 --> 00:11:31,820
Thus we unwind the defect that happened during winding.

114
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:42,580
And further don't forget at the subsequent start of winding to switch the system back to summing mode.

115
00:11:43,680 --> 00:11:45,320
Repeat the same operation.

116
00:11:46,120 --> 00:11:46,960
Our 40 turns.

117
00:11:48,100 --> 00:11:48,560
Add 1.

118
00:11:50,480 --> 00:11:55,020
And at subsequent system, start the calculator will count in a larger direction.

119
00:11:57,580 --> 00:12:02,220
This is the important moment that we count the turn count the coil frame did for us.

120
00:12:04,760 --> 00:12:08,900
To know precisely the number of meters of wire that was laid on this frame.

121
00:12:09,560 --> 00:12:12,340
This will be useful at the moment of frequency fitting.

122
00:12:13,320 --> 00:12:18,280
Combining LC resonance frequency with the frequency of the wave on conductor length.

123
00:12:18,940 --> 00:12:25,000
An important addition to this machine's build is the frame on which the spool with winding wire will rotate.

124
00:12:26,460 --> 00:12:33,340
This is the simplest variant, made in a quick hand, but nevertheless its effectiveness doesn't decrease from this.

125
00:12:33,940 --> 00:12:38,820
The sense is in setting a spool so its lower cheeks rubbed against the lower surface of the board.

126
00:12:42,980 --> 00:12:46,940
That way, due to friction force, we provide a certain wire tension.

127
00:12:48,300 --> 00:12:49,280
Here it slips.

128
00:12:50,760 --> 00:12:54,620
The places where the spool frame rubs during rotation are visible.

129
00:12:55,740 --> 00:13:02,900
If this moment isn't provided, then it'll be very hard during winding wire to hold so the turns wouldn't go off.

130
00:13:03,200 --> 00:13:07,320
Because you'd need to constantly use the second hand as a tensioning mechanism.

131
00:13:09,320 --> 00:13:12,260
And with such a method the process is much simpler.

132
00:13:12,820 --> 00:13:19,440
In the best case, of course, here on the build provides some additional friction, adjustable, but this is for the future.

133
00:13:20,700 --> 00:13:23,960
For now for winding a couple coils such a variant is quite working.

134
00:13:25,600 --> 00:13:29,360
Here I made holes for a plastic guide at a certain height needed by me.

135
00:13:31,220 --> 00:13:34,000
And this whole system performed quite well in work.

136
00:13:34,860 --> 00:13:40,080
Before starting to wind, the coil frame is dry wiped if there's any contamination on the surface.

